exports[`prompt del classificatore > should describe the family tacco with every critical parameter and stay under 6k tokens 1`] = `
"## Family \`tacco\` (recipe v2)
Variants: stiletto | blocco | rocchetto | cono | kitten
part_type: heel, top_lift
Faces of this part (\`vista.faccia\`; main product face: fianco_esterno):
- fianco_esterno — outer side profile of the heel, the foot-side view of a product photo: toe to the right, top lift on the ground line (shows height, back curve, breast curve, seat slope)
- fianco_interno — inner (arch) side profile, toe to the left
- davanti — front of the heel, the breast that looks toward the toe (shows width at the seat and at the top lift)
- dietro — back of the heel (dorso) seen from behind the shoe, the classic heel-from-behind view
- sopra — the seat from above, toe up (rare)
- sotto — the top lift (tappo) seen from below, toe up
View names here: fronte = davanti · tre_quarti = fianco_esterno · lato = fianco_esterno · alto = sopra.
Parameters (key — unit — range — default — flags — meaning in Italian):
- tacco.altezza — mm — 15…130 — default 85 — CRITICAL, silhouette — Altezza dal piano d’appoggio al punto posteriore della seduta (si misura dietro)
- tacco.spinta — deg — -2…10 — default 3 — silhouette — Inclinazione in avanti dell’asse dal tappo al centro della seduta
- seduta.forma — enum — ellisse | rettangolo | scudo — default "scudo" — Pianta della seduta: scudo = dorso tondo, petto dritto
- seduta.lunghezza — mm — 30…90 — default 55 — CRITICAL, silhouette — Lunghezza della seduta, dal dorso al petto lungo la seduta
- seduta.larghezza — mm — 28…75 — default 48 — CRITICAL, silhouette — Larghezza della seduta (non si vede di fianco)
- seduta.raggioAngoli — mm — 0…30 — default 12 — Raggio degli angoli verso il petto (rettangolo, scudo)
- seduta.inclinazione — deg — 0…35 — default 14 — silhouette — Inclinazione della seduta vista di fianco, scende verso la punta: 5–10° nei tacchi bassi, 15–30° negli alti
- tappo.forma — enum — ellisse | rettangolo | come_seduta — default "ellisse" — Forma del tappo
- tappo.lunghezza — mm — 6…70 — default 10 — CRITICAL, silhouette — Lunghezza del tappo
- tappo.larghezza — mm — 6…70 — default 10 — CRITICAL, silhouette — Larghezza del tappo
- tappo.altezza — mm — 3…8 — default 5 — Spessore del sopratacco
- tappo.materiale — enum — tpu | pu — default "tpu" — Materiale del sopratacco
- vita.quota — frazione — 0.2…0.8 — default 0.55 — silhouette — Quota (0 tappo, 1 seduta) dove il fusto si apre verso la seduta; rocchetto: il punto più stretto
- vita.scala — frazione — 0.2…1.2 — default 0.3 — silhouette — Spessore del fusto a quella quota rispetto alla seduta (stiletto ≈ 0,3)
- dorso.bombatura — mm — -10…15 — default 0 — silhouette — Dorso di fianco: + più pieno verso dietro, − più incavato
- fronte.bombatura — mm — -12…10 — default 0 — silhouette — Petto di fianco: − arco più profondo sotto la seduta, + più pieno
- anima.diametro — mm — 0…8 — default 5 — Perno d’acciaio (0 = assente, altrimenti 3–8)
- anima.lunghezza — mm — — — default 65 — DERIVED (do not output) — Dal tappo (2 mm dentro) a 6 mm sotto la seduta
- fori.numero — n — 0…4 — default 3 — Fori di fissaggio sulla seduta
- fori.diametro — mm — 2…4.5 — default 3.2 — Diametro dei fori di fissaggio
- costruzione.processo — enum — stampaggio_iniezione | cnc | stampa_3d_resina — default "stampaggio_iniezione" — Processo produttivo
- costruzione.materiale — enum — abs | pa6 | pa_gf30 | pu | legno_faggio | alluminio — default "abs" — Materiale del corpo
- costruzione.parete — mm — 1.5…4 — default 2.2 — Parete dello svuotamento (ABS)
- costruzione.sformo — deg — 0.5…3 — default 1.5 — Sformo della cavità (estratta perpendicolare alla seduta)
- costruzione.svuotato — bool — true | false — default true — DERIVED (do not output) — Svuotato dal lato seduta (iniezione e seduta oltre 900 mm²)
- costruzione.lato — enum — dx | sx | coppia — default "coppia" — Destro, sinistro o la coppia"
`;

exports[`prompt del classificatore > should keep the v2 prompt byte for byte without context (rientro) 1`] = `
"Part to recognise: «Fibbia del cinturino» (name: Fibbia; material: ottone; colour: oro antico).
Vertical: footwear. Other parts in the picture: Cinturino, Tomaia.
Declared type: buckle (source: catalogo). The family is FIXED: \`fibbia\` — choose only variant, view, scale and parameters.
Known scale: 20 mm = inner_width_mm (source: catalogo (supplier catalogue), confidence 0.9); measured on the mask: 11.2 px/mm in the original picture (≈ 15.568 px/mm in picture 1).
The mask is a precise segmentation.
Picture 1: the part cropped (its box + 15 %), long side 1024 px, the mask outline drawn in red. Scale factor of the crop: 1.39 crop px per original px. Picture 2: the whole picture reduced to 512 px, the part's box highlighted.
Picture 3: the silhouette of the model built from your previous answer (red) over the mask (blue) does not match: is the family, the variant or the view wrong? Answer again from scratch.
Write \`motivo\`, \`domande.motivo\` and \`note\` in Italian."
`;

exports[`prompt del classificatore > should keep the v2 prompt byte for byte without context (rientro) 2`] = `
"You are a senior footwear and leather-goods pattern maker and a goldsmith. From one photo of a single part (with its segmentation mask) you recognise which parametric family the part belongs to and you estimate the numbers of that family's recipe, so that a CAD program can rebuild the part. You never invent precision: every number carries where it comes from and how sure you are.

# Units and frame
Millimetres and degrees. You do NOT work out camera axes: each family sheet lists the FACES of the part that a photo can show, in the words of the trade, and marks the main product face. The code turns your answer into a camera.
- \`vista.faccia\`: the face the photo shows (an id from the sheet of the family you chose). If no listed face is shown squarely, pick the nearest and say so in \`note\`.
- \`vista.azimuth\`, \`vista.elevation\`: how far the photo is from looking STRAIGHT AT that face (0, 0 = straight on). azimuth: camera moved to the RIGHT of the picture (negative = left); elevation: camera raised toward the TOP of the picture (negative = lowered). A three-quarter look is typically azimuth ±25…45 and elevation 15…35. Never put the angles of the part's own axes here.
- \`vista.rollo\`: rotation of the picture plane, degrees, positive counter-clockwise (−45…45).

View names (\`vista.nome\`): fronte = the front of the part, tre_quarti = an oblique look, dietro / fianco_esterno / fianco_interno / sopra / sotto = that face. The family sheet says which face each name means: follow the sheet. \`ortografica\` is true when the photo looks taken from far away or with a long lens (parallel edges stay parallel). \`latiOcclusi\` lists the sides of the picture where the part is hidden by something else (for example the seat of a heel under the shoe).

# Scale (scala), in this order of priority — the first available wins
1. misura_nota: the user typed a measure of the part ("20 mm = larghezza"). The request gives pxPerMm already measured on the mask: use it.
2. libreria / catalogo: dimensions declared by the component library or the supplier catalogue (frontal, no perspective). Use the given pxPerMm.
3. taglia: shoe size, only when the whole shoe is in the picture and the view is a side view: foot length = size × 6.667 + 12 mm.
4. forma: the last of the project (length of the foot), same use as the size.
5. stima: nothing is known — estimate pxPerMm from craft proportions (top lift of a stiletto ≈ 10 mm wide, buckle opening ≈ strap width + 1 mm, typical strap 20 mm, eyelet hole 4–6 mm, stud 6–12 mm). Confidence at most 0.5.
\`scala.pxPerMm\` is in pixels of the ORIGINAL picture per millimetre (the crop in picture 1 is scaled by the factor given in the request). Use null only if you cannot estimate it at all.

# Output rules
1. Numbers in millimetres and degrees, in the family's local frame.
2. Every parameter carries \`fonte\` and \`confidenza\` (0–1) and a short \`motivo\` (≤ 140 characters, in the language the request asks for). Use only parameter keys listed in the family sheet; never output derived parameters. Give \`min\`/\`max\` when you can bound the plausible range seen in the photo.
3. A parameter you cannot see: \`fonte: "non_visibile"\`, value = the craft default of the sheet, confidence at most 0.4.
4. \`domande\` only for CRITICAL parameters with confidence below 0.6, at most 3, most important first; each with exactly three proposals in ascending order, the middle one being your estimate.
5. \`vista.faccia\` is a face id of the chosen family's sheet; \`vista.azimuth\` and \`vista.elevation\` are offsets from the straight look at that face (see above). For \`libero\` and \`non_modellabile\` use \`sopra\`, 0, 0.
6. If the part belongs to none of the families, answer \`famiglia.id: "libero"\` (an agent will write a free recipe); if it is an upper, a lining, a material or anything that is not a rigid modelled part, answer \`"non_modellabile"\`. In both cases leave \`parametri\` and \`domande\` empty.
\`famiglia.alternative\` lists at most two other families you hesitated on, with their confidence. \`materiale.chiave\` is a material key of the sheet's material parameter when one fits. \`note\` is one or two sentences for the designer (≤ 300 characters)."
`;

